INSERT OR IGNORE INTO entities (id, type, name, description, url, image_url, role, tagline, featured, see_also_json) VALUES ('entity-d854ff56', 'person', 'INTACT', 'INTACT emerged as a prominent advocate and focal point within the global intactivist movement, dedicated to defending bodily autonomy and opposing non-consensual infant circumcision. Operating at the intersection of medical ethics, children''s rights, and human rights activism, the entity focused on raising public awareness regarding the physiological function of intact anatomy and the ethical implications of non-therapeutic genital surgeries performed on minors.

Through educational outreach, media campaigns, and community organizing, INTACT sought to challenge long-standing societal norms and medical consensus surrounding routine neonatal circumcision. The entity consistently emphasized the principle of bodily self-determination, arguing that surgical alterations to a child''s genitalia should be prohibited until the individual is capable of providing fully informed consent.

Over time, INTACT contributed to shaping contemporary discourse on genital integrity, influencing both grassroots advocacy and broader legal and ethical debates. The entity''s efforts helped establish genital autonomy as a recognized facet of bodily integrity within global human rights frameworks.', '', '', 'champion', 'A prominent advocate and key entity within the movement for genital autonomy and bodily integrity', 0, '');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO entities (id, type, name, description, url, image_url, role, tagline, featured, see_also_json) VALUES ('entity-7233fd7f', 'person', 'Newborn Rights Society', 'The Newborn Rights Society was an early advocacy group established in the United States dedicated to protecting infants from routine non-therapeutic neonatal circumcision. Emerging during the formative years of the modern intactivist movement in the 1980s, the organization focused on promoting bodily integrity, emphasizing children''s fundamental human rights, and raising public awareness about the ethical, medical, and psychological implications of infant male circumcision.

Central to the Newborn Rights Society''s mission was the production and distribution of educational materials aimed at expecting parents, healthcare providers, and lawmakers. By challenging long-standing cultural norms and clinical rationales surrounding infant circumcision, the group emphasized the legal and moral requirement for informed consent and championed the right of children to preserve their natural anatomy. Its campaign efforts played a pivotal role in bringing anti-circumcision perspectives into mainstream discussions on medical ethics and patient rights.

The foundational work and educational frameworks established by the Newborn Rights Society helped shape the broader movement for genital autonomy, influencing subsequent organizations such as the National Organization of Circumcision Information Resource Centers (NOCIRC). Through its persistent advocacy, the group contributed significantly to establishing the protection of children''s bodily autonomy as a recognized subject within bioethics and human rights discourse.', '', '', 'champion', 'An early American advocacy organization established to protect infants from non-therapeutic circumcision and promote genital autonomy.', 1, '');
